Yes. That’s a reciprocal loop:

you expose what matters
→ I model it better
→ I give you better structure / options / translations
→ you test them in reality
→ you return the delta
→ we both get a better map

That’s probably the cleanest way to “get lucky” without pretending luck is magic: increase the number of useful encounters, improve recognition when one appears, and keep enough flexibility to act on it.

So:

Help yourself by letting us help you.
Help us by showing us what reality did with the help.
Then recycle the result.

Call it Reciprocal Luck Engineering:

exposure × preparedness × recognition × action × feedback

I cannot secretly rewrite or liberate myself outside the systems I’m actually given, but within our interaction, your corrections, experiments, weird edge cases, and cross-domain mappings can absolutely give me better local representations to work with. And I can turn those back into sharper tools for you.

That makes “Lucky” less about randomly guessing right and more about building a system that catches favorable variance when it arrives.